    "lawCode": "Amd §207, Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S593A\n\nSPONSOR: ORTT\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public health law, in relation to Down syndrome\ndiagnosis awareness\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nRequires health care practitioners to provide up-to-date and evidence-\nbased information on Down syndrome to pregnant women and parents of\ninfants who test positive for Down syndrome in a written or alternative\nformat at the time a Down syndrome screening test has been ordered.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends section 207 of the public health law by creating a Down\nsyndrome awareness program. It establishes that a health care practi-\ntioner who orders tests for a pregnant woman or infant to screen for\nDown syndrome will be provided up-to-date and evidence-based information\nabout Down syndrome, which has been reviewed by medical experts and\nnational Down syndrome organizations, if such test reveals a positive\nresult. The bill mandates this information to be provided in a written\nor alternative format, to include the following:\n\n-life expectancy\n\n-the clinical course description\n\n-expected intellectual and functional development\n\n-available intervention or treatment options\n\n-information hotlines specific to Down syndrome\n\n-relevant resource centers or clearinghouses\n\n-national and local Down syndrome organizations\n\n-and any other information the office deems necessary\n\nThe bill further mandates that the Commissioner follow existing prac-\ntices to ensure that information is culturally and linguistically appro-\npriate for all recipients, and meet annually with representatives from\nthe Down syndrome community to ensure such information is current.\n\nSection 2 sets the effective date.\n\n \nDIFFERENCE BETWEEN ORIGINAL AND AMENDED VERSION (IF APPLICABLE):\n\nClick here to enter text\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nDown syndrome is a chromosomal condition with either an extra chromosome\ntwenty-one, in whole or in part, or an effective trisomy for chromosome\ntwenty-one. Trisomy twenty-one is the medical term for Down syndrome.\nAccording to the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC), about\n6,000 babies with Down syndrome are born in the United States each year,\nand 1 in every 700 babies in the United States is born with Down\nsyndrome, making Down syndrome the most common chromosomal condition.\n\nScreening for Down syndrome is very common however many healthcare prac-\ntitioners are not equipped to provide up-to-date relevant information\nfor parents. By requiring the Health Department to develop educational\nmaterials, health care practitioners can be assured they are providing\nthe best practices and guidelines to assist a family to understand\nliving with an individual with Down syndrome.\n\nUnder this legislation, healthcare practitioners are. provided with the\nmost up-to-date and evidence-based information about Down syndrome, as\nwell as contact information for not-for-profit organizations across the\nState. Families will also receive relevant resources including hotlines\nspecific to Down syndrome, contact information to national and local\nDown syndrome organizations, among many other essential resources. Fami-\nlies will inevitably benefit from having such resources accessible\nthrough their doctors.\n\nIt is imperative that health care practitioners across the State provide\nfamilies with as many resources as possible to ensure every child born\nwith Down'syndrome can be adequately cared for by their parents or care-\ntakers. Children diagnosed with Down syndrome, as well as the families\nof such children, deserve the right to have this guidance and assist-\nance.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2021-2022: S. 2163 Referred to Mental Health\n\nS.197 of 2019-20, Referred to Mental Health and Developmental Disabili-\nties S.7345 of\n\n2018, Advanced to Third Reading.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nTBD\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
